($t$Ceiba aesculifolia$t$, $t$Ceiba aesculifolia$t$, $t$Malvaceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Malvaceae. Portamento: Tree. Altezza massima indicativa: 25 m. Found in Central America, Pochote (Ceiba aesculifolia) is a tropical deciduous tree with a thick, short bole and spreading crown. It reaches up to 25 m tall. Young fruit and ripe seeds are edible when cooked - ripe fruits are stewed and seeds are roasted. Seedpods are sources of floss that has various uses like as stuffing material in pillows, toys, and cushions, as tinder for starting fires, as material in weaving covers, and as an insulating material in refrigerators.  Found In: Central America, Costa Rica, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras, Mexico, Nicaragua, North America. Other names: Ceibillo, Pochotte ceiba, Pochote apochote, Ceiba, Puchote.$t$, $j${"luce": "Pieno sole", "acqua": "Moderata", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: sabbioso/leggero, medio, argilloso/pesante"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Pochote$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): None known$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "npk": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}}$j$::jsonb, $j${"famiglia_botanica": "Malvaceae", "giorni_germinazione": null, "giorni_trapianto": null, "giorni_raccolta": null, "finestra_semina": [], "finestra_trapianto": [], "resistenza_gelo": "bassa", "spaziatura_cm": null, "consociazioni_favorevoli": [], "consociazioni_sfavorevoli": []}$j$::jsonb, $j${"adatta": null, "dimensione_minima_litri": null, "rinvaso_ogni_mesi": null, "irrigazione_fattore": null}$j$::jsonb, $t$perenne$t$, $t$ceiba-aesculifolia$t$, ARRAY[$t$Plants For A Future (pfaf.org) — importazione bozza automatica, dati non verificati manualmente$t$], $t$bozza$t$, null, null),
($t$Ceiba pentandra$t$, $t$Ceiba pentandra$t$, $t$Malvaceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Malvaceae. Portamento: Tree. Altezza massima indicativa: 50 m. A tropical tree native to Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean, northern South America, and to tropical west Africa, Kapok Tree or Ceiba pentandra is now a widely cultivated in Southeast Asia for its seed fibre. It is fast growing and reaches up to 70 m in height and 3 m in trunk diameter. The trunk is buttressed and large simple thorns, similar to that of on many of the larger branches, are found on it. The crown is thin and pagoda shaped. The leaves are palmate, with 5 to 9 leaflets each. Kapok produces several pods that contain seeds covered by fibre. Kapok fibre is yellowish in colour, light, very buoyant, water resistant, resilient, but very flammable. It is used as stuffing in mattresses, pillows, etc. The seed of kapok yields oil that is used in making soap and as fertilizer. The seed is edible either raw or cooked (roasted and ground into powder). Tender leaves, buds, and fruits are eaten like Abelmoschus moschatus or okra. The flowers are blanched and eaten with chilli sauce; dried stamens are added to curries and soups. Bark decoction, on the other hand, is used as a diuretic, aphrodisiac, and as treatment for headache and diabetes. Kapok, in general, is used medicinally for fever, spasms, and bleeding. Different plant parts have a wide range of medicinal functions as well.$t$, $j${"luce": "Pieno sole", "acqua": "Moderata", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: medio, argilloso/pesante"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Kapok Tree, Cotton Tree, Suma'ma$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): The seeds, and the oil, contain cyclopropenoid fatty acids such as malvalic acid (7 - 8%) and sterculic acid (3%), which cause abnormal physiological reactions in animals. Therefore the consumption of kapok seeds or seed oil should be discouraged unless the cyclopropenoid acids have been chemically removed[ 299 ]. Kapok fibre is irritating to the eyes, nose and throat, and workers exposed to kapok dust for long periods may develop chronic bronchitis[ 299 ]. (Plant has spines or sharp edges; use extreme caution when handling )$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "npk": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}}$j$::jsonb, $j${"famiglia_botanica": "Malvaceae", "giorni_germinazione": null, "giorni_trapianto": null, "giorni_raccolta": null, "finestra_semina": [], "finestra_trapianto": [], "resistenza_gelo": "bassa", "spaziatura_cm": null, "consociazioni_favorevoli": [], "consociazioni_sfavorevoli": []}$j$::jsonb, $j${"adatta": null, "dimensione_minima_litri": null, "rinvaso_ogni_mesi": null, "irrigazione_fattore": null}$j$::jsonb, $t$perenne$t$, $t$ceiba-pentandra$t$, ARRAY[$t$Plants For A Future (pfaf.org) — importazione bozza automatica, dati non verificati manualmente$t$], $t$bozza$t$, null, null),
